Kevaundray Wedderburn: Okay, so we put out a post recently that is proposing to use Gethack256 as the hash function for our hash-based cryptography. So this will include LeanVM, but also Lean XMSS, which is the signature scheme on the CL, and Lean Sphinx, which is the signature scheme on the EL.

626
01:38:02.890 --> 01:38:17.400
Kevaundray Wedderburn: At the end of the post, I think there's a part that says, like, how does it affect, like, EL Devs or client devs? For the EL, it basically doesn't affect you that much, since you're already using Kak 256.

627
01:38:17.610 --> 01:38:28.209
Kevaundray Wedderburn: But there is a proposal to use for frame transactions to compute the transaction hash using SSZ, and I would suggest to use SSZ with Gethack there.

628
01:38:28.300 --> 01:38:41.749
Kevaundray Wedderburn: For the CL, yeah, it's basically just a signature scheme that changes, so it's partly opaque to you, but if we do… like, in the future, we could switch to SSZ with Keccak there as well, but it's not something you need for PQ.

629
01:38:41.880 --> 01:38:45.380
Kevaundray Wedderburn: So happy to answer more questions about this change.

630
01:38:52.050 --> 01:38:53.160
Ben Edgington: Questions for Kev?

631
01:38:57.700 --> 01:39:02.190
Kevaundray Wedderburn: We also have some of the cryptographers on here, Benedict.

632
01:39:06.490 --> 01:39:07.470
Ben Edgington: Go on, Felix.

633
01:39:08.480 --> 01:39:10.490
Felix (Geth): Yeah, and just quickly…

634
01:39:10.510 --> 01:39:23.509
Felix (Geth): interested to ask why we are sticking to this hash function, since it is not standardized. So, at least not in the parameter set that we are using in the EL, and this has caused us some issue over time.

635
01:39:23.530 --> 01:39:32.349
Felix (Geth): So, I'm curious why it was chosen again, or why we are not choosing a standardized hash function parameter set.

636
01:39:33.590 --> 01:39:46.320
Kevaundray Wedderburn: Right, yeah, so I think I wrote that at somewhere in the bottom, but essentially, we're not using anything that's standardized, and Gethack is already integrated into the EL quite heavily, so

637
01:39:46.800 --> 01:39:56.770
Kevaundray Wedderburn: doesn't seem like there's a strong, argument to standardize… to use something standardized like SHA-3, and SHA-3 change… the change between SHA-3 and Keccak is, like, a padding byte.

638
01:39:59.500 --> 01:40:10.460
Felix (Geth): Yeah, but it has caused us a lot of pain, because, like, the software implementation of this hash function, we always have to modify to make sure that we can support the standard which no one else is

639
01:40:11.080 --> 01:40:12.130
Felix (Geth): using…

640
01:40:12.540 --> 01:40:28.190
Felix (Geth): So, I mean, we could also go with SHA-256 or any of the other standardized ones. I just find it a bit interesting that now we're kind of discussing to, like, move everything to this one function that is basically not very well supported across the software ecosystem. There is always a way, but…

641
01:40:28.310 --> 01:40:30.070
Felix (Geth): It's just… interesting to me.

642
01:40:30.810 --> 01:40:47.120
Dmitry Khovratovich: I think it's actually worth noticing that we don't use just a hash function. We use more than a hash function, for example, in various proofs for hash image transformation, we need something more. We need what's called a duplex sponge.

643
01:40:47.180 --> 01:40:55.539
Dmitry Khovratovich: And inside this duplex sponge, there is a Keccak permutation, the core of the… both Gethack hash and SHA-3 hash.

644
01:40:55.540 --> 01:41:11.870
Dmitry Khovratovich: So having a Shaft rehash alone wouldn't help us implementing a duplex sponge. We would need bigger functionality anyway, and we are building on the same permutation that is used inside Kakak and inside possible duplex sponges that could

645
01:41:11.870 --> 01:41:19.869
Dmitry Khovratovich: be built on the Keccak permutation. So, in fact, we're not deviating that much, and SHA-3 alone wouldn't be sufficient anyway.

646
01:41:22.020 --> 01:41:26.880
Felix (Geth): It was more… my comment was more about just, like, trying to…

647
01:41:27.040 --> 01:41:42.879
Felix (Geth): if we have the chance to choose something in the high level, I agree with the sponge construction, it's the same, but if we have now talking about converting the SSZ hashing to catch up, it seems a bit strange to me. I just wanted to raise this, but I'm done.

648
01:41:43.520 --> 01:41:48.980
Ben Edgington: Considerably raised, thank you, Felix. Yeah, one, time for a very quick remark, Kev.

649
01:41:49.410 --> 01:42:04.200
Kevaundray Wedderburn: Yeah, I was just gonna say that there are places where we actually can't get rid of Keccak as easily, like, the Xcode hash function is probably not gonna change anytime soon, and, like, addresses as well. So Keccak is likely here to just stay forever.

650
01:42:05.870 --> 01:42:20.560
Ben Edgington: Fair point. Alright, more feedback to Kev, please, and the blog post is up on the EF blog, usual place. And thank you, everybody, for a productive call. Look forward to making some more decisions in two weeks' time.

651
01:42:20.970 --> 01:42:25.239
Ben Edgington: I'll see you on Discord and elsewhere. Breakout calls galore.

652
01:42:25.460 --> 01:42:27.449
Ben Edgington: Alright, thanks all. Bye-bye.

653
01:42:28.900 --> 01:42:29.670
soispoke: Thank you.

654
01:42:32.290 --> 01:42:33.140
Mikhail Kalinin: Thank you.

655
01:42:33.190 --> 01:42:34.899
Toni Wahrstätter: Thank you, bye-bye.

